  "answer_text": "**altavista.com** was the web address of **AltaVista**, one of the most popular and pioneering search engines of the early internet era. \n\nIf you type `altavista.com` into your browser today, it will simply redirect you to **Yahoo! Search**. \n\nHere is a look back at the history, rise, and eventual fall of this internet legend:\n\n### 1. The Rise: A Tech Powerhouse (1995\u20131997)\n* **The Launch:** AltaVista was launched on December 15, 1995, by researchers at Digital Equipment Corporation (DEC). It was originally designed to showcase the processing power of DEC's Alpha computers.\n* **Revolutionary Tech:** It was the first search engine to provide a **\"full-text\" searchable index** of the World Wide Web, allowing users to search for any word on any indexed page using advanced boolean operators (like AND, OR, NOT).\n* **Speed and Scale:** Powered by a highly efficient web crawler named \"Scooter,\" AltaVista was much faster than its competitors (like Lycos, WebCrawler, and Magellan) and indexed tens of millions of pages when others indexed only a fraction. \n* **Babel Fish:** In 1997, AltaVista launched Babel Fish, the web's first widely used, real-time machine translation service, allowing users to instantly translate text and entire web pages.\n\n### 2. The Misstep: Becoming a \"Portal\"\nIn 1998, Compaq bought DEC, acquiring AltaVista in the process. During the late '90s \"dot-com bubble,\" Compaq attempted to transform AltaVista from a clean, lightning-fast search box into an all-in-one **web portal** (similar to Yahoo! at the time). \n\nThey added shopping, email, news, chat rooms, and a massive amount of visual clutter to the homepage. This backfired:\n* It alienated users who preferred the clean, focused search interface.\n* It slowed down load times.\n* Most crucially, it opened the door for a brand-new competitor with a radically simple, search-only homepage and superior ranking technology: **Google**.\n\n### 3. The Fall and Acquisitions\n* **The Google Effect:** As Google's minimalist interface and superior \"PageRank\" algorithm took over the internet, AltaVista rapidly lost its user base.\n* **Overture and Yahoo:** In 2003, AltaVista was sold to Overture Services, which was purchased by Yahoo! later that year. Yahoo! eventually discontinued AltaVista's independent search technology, replacing it behind the scenes with Yahoo!'s own search engine.\n\n### 4. The End of an Era\nOn **July 8, 2013**, Yahoo! officially retired the AltaVista brand after 18 years of operation, redirecting the domain to Yahoo! Search. \n\nWhile it is gone, AltaVista's pioneering contributions to web crawling, indexing, and translation laid the technical foundation for the search engines we use every day.",
